<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kalka–Shimla Railway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1903">1903</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalka">Kalka</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a> Railway is a narrow-gauge mountain railway in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, opened in 1903 to link Kalka on the plains with Shimla, the summer capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>. About 96 km long, it climbs through more than 100 tunnels and over more than 800 bridges. It is part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> of the Mountain Railways of India.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalka-shimla-railway.jpg" alt="Kalka–Shimla Railway" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AHEMSLTD · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KSR_Steam_special_at_Taradevi_05-02-13_56.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The idea of connecting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a> by rail was first raised by a correspondent to the Delhi Gazette in November 1847. Shimla became the summer capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> in 1864 and the headquarters of the Indian army, so twice a year the entire government had to be moved between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> and Shimla by horse- and ox-drawn carts. The opening of the broad-gauge Delhi–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalka">Kalka</a> line in 1891 made a branch to Shimla feasible. Surveys were made in 1884, 1885, 1892, 1893 and 1895, and after much debate an adhesion line was chosen in preference to a rack system.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Construction was begun by the privately funded Delhi-Ambala-Kalka Railway Company after a contract with the secretary of state on 29 June 1898. The government gave the land free but no financial aid. Herbert Septimus Harington was chief engineer and Sujan Singh Hadaliwale chief contractor. The line opened on 9 November 1903 and was dedicated by Viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon">Lord Curzon</a>. The estimated cost of 8,678,500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a> had doubled by the opening. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a> thought the 2 ft gauge too narrow, so the contract was revised on 15 November 1901 and the line was regauged to 2 ft 6 in.</p><p>The route climbs from 656 metres at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalka">Kalka</a> to 2,075 metres at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a>, through 20 stations and more than a hundred tunnels, the longest at Barog. The first locomotives were two small engines from the Darjeeling Himalayan Railway, replaced from 1902 by ten 0-4-2T engines built by Sharp, Stewart and Company. Thirty larger 2-6-2T engines followed between 1904 and 1910. In 1908 the coaches were rebuilt with aluminium roofs, an early use of aluminium in coach construction, so that locomotives could haul six coaches instead of four. Regular steam operation ended in 1971, and diesel locomotives began running in 1955.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The company had spent 16,525,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a> by 1904 with no sign of profit, and the government bought the line on 1 January 1906 for 17,107,748 rupees. In 2007 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a> government declared the railway a heritage property, and on 8 July 2008 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> added it to the mountain railways of India World Heritage Site, alongside the Darjeeling Himalayan and Nilgiri Mountain Railways. The BBC Four series Indian Hill Railways featured it in 2010. Several sections were washed out by landslides during the 2023 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-india">North India</a> floods.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kalka–Shimla Railway” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>With the coming of the <b>Kalka-Shimla</b> road and railway, Solan became its capital and grew as a market town.</blockquote><a href="/state/baghat-state">Baghat State</a></li></ul></section>
